data.content:
This is a well-documented **C# 14 breaking change** related to new span overload resolution.

## What happened

In C# 14 / .NET 10, new built-in conversions from arrays to `Span<T>` / `ReadOnlySpan<T>` cause overload resolution to prefer `MemoryExtensions.Contains<T>(this ReadOnlySpan<T>, T)` over `Enumerable.Contains<T>(this IEnumerable<T>, T)`.

The `MemoryExtensions.Contains` overload **cannot be represented in an expression tree**, so this throws an `InvalidOperationException` at runtime when the expression is compiled with `preferInterpretation: true` (or at compile time in other contexts).

## How to fix

Cast the array to `IEnumerable<int>` or use `.AsEnumerable()` to force the compiler to select `Enumerable.Contains`:

```csharp
// Option 1: .AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 2: Explicit static call
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);

// Option 3: Cast
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);
```

All three force binding to `Enumerable.Contains`, which expression trees support.
